# Build the human page editor

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical notes below describe observed implementation gaps, which the
finite theoretical contract does not silently resolve.

Support selection, text correction, drag, resize, crop, align, group, layer,
duplicate, delete, and style changes through typed semantic commands.

## Finite typed page-edit command contract

**Inputs:** one current accepted notebook revision, selected page/object ID,
its semantic kind, expected current field values and user-proposed edit.
**Outputs:** an immutable command preview and, after explicit authorization,
either a new accepted revision with a typed change receipt or an exact refusal.
The preview must not directly mutate the canvas, session or physical device.

**Admitted first-release targets:** text corrections target one existing text
span, and geometry/layer changes target one fixed figure whose original asset
reference is already admitted. Every requested mutation carries its exact
base revision and identity preconditions. Coordinate edits use physical mm
in the accepted page coordinate system, not guessed device pixels. No editor
operation provides filesystem paths or hardware arming/start authority.

**Worked command paths:** given revision `r10`, text span `t1` (`Monday`),
and fixed figure `f1` at `(30,40)` mm with size `20 x 15` mm, propose:

| UI intent | Typed semantic effect / first-release boundary |
| --- | --- |
| Correct text | Replace `t1` text with `Tuesday`, preserving `t1` |
| Move | Set `f1` position to `(35,40)` mm |
| Resize | Set `f1` size to `25 x 15` mm |
| Crop | Set `f1` clip rectangle to `(0,0,18,15)` mm |
| Align | Set `f1` left anchor to admitted margin at `20` mm |
| Reorder | Put `f1` in `below-text` layer at explicit z-order `0` |
| Duplicate | Allocate a new figure `f2` referencing the same asset |
| Delete | Remove only `f2` after a valid duplicate is accepted |

Each example is a distinct proposed transaction against the version produced
by its predecessor when applied sequentially. A grouped set of operations
may apply only after every target and constraint passes backend validation;
otherwise none applies. Duplicate creates an independently addressable
semantic identity and never copies raw asset bytes into the command.

**Refusal example:** a crop larger than original bounds, a blocked page edge,
a stale `r10` base, an inadmissible semantic kind, or a missing asset refuses
the complete affected batch and leaves the accepted notebook untouched.
Advisory alignment is not an automatic repair for collisions or page overflow;
a changed placement requires fresh diagnostics before Export readiness.

**Assumptions and limits:** exact-base direct semantic edits and the command
family vocabulary exist for limited current targets. Human pointer gestures,
spatial constraint solving, crop/render evaluation, grouping, style changes,
complete duplicate/delete flows, and browser transaction transport remain
unavailable. The examples are a theoretical design, not evidence that the
human page editor can already execute all eight command paths.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** theoretical for all eight bounded UI-to-command paths, with
  partial practical direct-edit primitives, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** finite text/figure targets, eight typed examples, revision
  and owner invariants, atomic refusal, and excluded physical/file authority.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** complete human page editing,
  canvas gesture adapter, solver, grouping/style UI, or Export integration.
